                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "21412449",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198121351603",
                            "num_reviews": 7,
                            "playtime_forever": 142,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 0,
                            "playtime_at_review": 49,
                            "last_played": 1457466445
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "In Mesel you play as a novelist investigating an abandoned children's hospital for information for your new novel. Now exploring an abandoned hospital isn't exactly new when it comes to horror games but its done well here. With just about everything you do in the game, the novel gets written and you can see what you have so far by pressing R. This is actually pretty cool but it gets old fairly fast as it updates so frequently that if you try to check it every time you get an update then you'll never get through the game and if you put it off too long then you end up with a crap ton to read, I don't know about you guys but I don't play games to read.\n\nIf you ignore the actual novel and just play the game then there's a lot to recommend. The graphics, while not world-beating, are very good and do a lot to create the atmosphere. The hospital is dark but not too dark. There are some technical problems (as with nearly every indie game) but none of them were enough to ruin the game. The story is very good. I won't spoil any of it here, but it really pushed me further into the game to find out what is going on.\n\nOne of the selling points of the game is that there is no combat. Don't take this to mean that you can't die. I have. Multiple times.\n\nAll in all, its a very spooky game. The story is very intriguing and told in a new way (the novel) and its definitely worth $5 (at time of writing) if you're into indie horror.",
